message.content:
{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement criticizes the administration's Dubai Ports deal and demands congressional review. This is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration under negative clarification (1), with no claim of having produced any favorable government action."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "A Pearl Harbor remembrance statement honoring service members. This is ceremonial content (commemoration/tribute) under negative clarification (5), with no government action or benefit claimed."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander announces receiving a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from an outside advocacy group based on his voting record. This is ceremonial/award content and a recitation of votes under negative clarifications (1) and (5); he claims no responsibility for a specific delivered government action."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb's statement responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush administration's foreign policy. Pure position-taking and criticism under negative clarification (1); no delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd comments on the Connecticut impact of Bush's proposed budget and advocates for two additional submarines per year. This is advocacy for future action and position-taking on a proposal under negative clarifications (1) and (3); no funded outcome or delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement on Iraq salutes Iraqi voters, criticizes the president's lack of a plan, and says Democrats will demand a strategy. This is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration under negative clarification (1); no benefit or government action is claimed as his accomplishment."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of a resolution honoring the late Senator Eagleton, which McCaskill co-introduced. This is ceremonial content—a commemoration/tribute to another person—under negative clarification (5). The resolution delivers no tangible government benefit, so its passage is not credit-claiming under clarification (3), which requires framing as a victory delivering benefits."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release partly explains Nelson's vote for the SCHIP bill, its headline and body emphasize that 'Nebraska's Senator's Small Business Health Care Amendment [was] Included'—the legislation the Senate passed 'includes an amendment co-sponsored by Senator Nelson.' Under clarification (3), announcing inclusion in advancing/passing legislation of a provision the senator sponsored, framed as a victory, counts as credit-claiming; as in the Lugar positive example, the inclusion itself is the claimed accomplishment even though the sense-of-the-senate provision's benefits are not yet realized. Negative clarification (2) does not apply because something has passed and his provision is included in it."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dayton 'applauded' Senate passage of additional LIHEAP funding while expressing disappointment it falls short. He claims no causal role in the funding—his own four amendments to fully fund LIHEAP were defeated. This matches negative clarification (4) (welcoming/applauding a favorable development without claiming credit) and (1) (position-taking), so it is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement urges completion of an asbestos trust-fund bill and recounts past progress, including the earlier adoption of the 'Leahy Hatch medical criteria amendment.' The document's purpose is advocacy/position-taking about unfinished legislation, and the reference to his past amendment is résumé-style recollection rather than an announcement of a delivered benefit. Under negative clarifications (1), (3), and (7), this is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration that the House will not take up the D.C. voting rights bill and commends others (Davis, Norton, Utah officials, Hatch, Bennett) for their efforts. This is position-taking and advocacy for future action under negative clarifications (1), (3), and (6); nothing has passed and he claims no delivered benefit."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is identical to the codebook's positive example: Lugar announces that provisions of his authored Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act 'will be included' in the Grassley-Baucus Katrina tax-relief package. Under clarification (3), announcing inclusion of one's authored provisions in advancing legislation, framed as delivering benefits, is credit-claiming even before enactment."
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Clinton announces that the Air Force will place a new Predator squadron in New York and frames it as an economic boon for the state. Under clarification (5), claiming credit for attracting military missions counts, and the clarification further provides that announcing a benefit for one's state counts even without the word 'secured,' because the announcement itself associates the senator with the delivered benefit. This is a final delivered decision, unlike the negative Clinton example where she merely welcomed a preliminary recommendation and urged further action."
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement mourns the passing of former Washington Democratic Party chair Karen Marchioro. This is a condolence/tribute, which is ceremonial content under negative clarification (5); no government action or benefit is involved."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces USDA's purchase of up to 80 million pounds of potatoes benefiting Idaho growers and explicitly notes that 'the Idaho Congressional Delegation wrote the Secretary requesting this action,' linking the favorable agency decision to the delegation's own request. Under clarification (1), announcing federal action benefiting one's state with reference to the senator's own letters/requests is credit-claiming; shared credit with the delegation still counts."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces a USDA disaster designation for Missouri and notes he 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval of the Governor's request,' plus references his earlier announcement of $1,078,500 in ECP funds. Under clarifications (1) and (2), announcing a disaster declaration benefiting his state and claiming his intervention expedited the favorable agency decision is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist comments on new job numbers by praising 'Republican economic policies' and attacking Democrats. This is position-taking that credits his party rather than himself under negative clarifications (1) and (6); he claims no personal responsibility for a specific government action."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Harkin expresses hope that a new regional diabetes facility will help Iowans, and the release notes he authored the HeLP America Act. There is no claim that he secured funding or produced the facility; he expresses hope about a development and the bill authorship is general résumé language not tied to a delivered benefit. Under negative clarifications (4) and (7), this is welcoming/advocacy, not credit-claiming. The truncated text shows no 'secured' or request-based claim."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush's call for comprehensive immigration reform and urges turning 'talk into action.' This is advocacy/position-taking urging future action under negative clarifications (1) and (3); no favorable outcome has occurred and no benefit is claimed."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces he has joined a bipartisan group supporting the White House PATRIOT Act compromise and explains his support. This is a vote/position explanation under negative clarification (1); he does not claim to have authored or secured any provision of the compromise, only that he is 'pleased' with it."
  }
}

{  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The document contains only a bare headline referencing a committee markup on venture capital and education bills, with no substantive text. There is no claim of responsibility for any delivered benefit, passed legislation, or favorable decision. It cannot be credit-claiming."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement advocates for cameras in federal courtrooms and criticizes the Bush administration's secrecy. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position-taking; the bills he mentions cosponsoring are merely pending, so under negative clarification (2) they are proposals, not accomplishments. No benefit has been delivered and no favorable outcome is claimed as his doing."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter requesting that the Air Force clarify religious-expression guidelines at the Academy. This is a request urging future agency action with no favorable outcome yet achieved, squarely within negative clarification (3) (advocacy, demands, or letters urging future action)."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that Medicare's late-enrollment penalty will be waived for hurricane-affected seniors, and frames the favorable decision as a response to Landrieu's pressure: the headline says the 'Administrator finally responds to Sen. Landrieu's letter,' and Landrieu says the agency 'finally come to its senses and recognized the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention.' This matches clarification (2), which expressly lists 'a waived penalty' as the kind of favorable agency decision produced by a senator's intervention or pressure that counts as credit-claiming."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Landrieu and Kerry call for local contracts in Katrina/Rita rebuilding and write the SBA to speed up loss verification. This is advocacy and a letter urging future action where no favorable outcome has yet occurred, excluded by negative clarification (3)."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "DeWine announces a $78,046 federal grant to the Troy Township (Ohio) Fire Department and explicitly ties the benefit to his own work: he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' and as an Appropriations member 'helped secure $650 million' for it. This matches clarification (1) (announcing federal grant funds benefiting his state with references to his own appropriations work) and clarification (3) (his authored law delivering benefits)."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a condolence statement on the death of Rep. Charlie Norwood, expressing sympathy and hoping Congress continues his legacy. Under negative clarification (5), condolences are ceremonial content, not credit-claiming; no government action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement at the CHIP enrollment ceremony urges the President not to veto the bill and praises the bipartisan work of Baucus, Rockefeller, Grassley, and Hatch. He claims no personal role in producing the benefit; this is position-taking and crediting others, excluded by negative clarifications (1) and (6)."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Domenici requests an expedited hearing and markup on a livestock-assistance bill he cosponsored, and notes earlier requests to USDA and the President for emergency aid. These are demands and letters urging future action on a bill that has not passed, falling under negative clarifications (2) and (3). No benefit has been delivered and no favorable outcome is claimed as his achievement."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig announces a specific favorable agency decision—expanded VA clinic services in Lewiston, Idaho—and frames it as a response to his own advocacy, thanking VA officials 'for their willingness to hear my concerns and—more importantly—respond to the needs of the veterans.' Under clarification (2), claiming his intervention produced a favorable agency decision counts, and the clarification also notes that announcing a benefit for one's state associates the senator with the delivered benefit even without the word 'secured.'"
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the main purpose is announcing a bill introduction (which alone would be excluded by negative clarification (2)), the release also contains an explicit credit claim: 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape, Sen. Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan... was able to come... to the United States for treatment.' This is a claim that her personal intervention produced a favorable government outcome for a specific beneficiary, matching clarification (2) of the positive criteria."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding to contain coqui frogs on the Big Island and explicitly claims personal responsibility: 'I then went looking and found another federal funding source to at least begin the control and containment process.' This is a paradigmatic announcement of federal funds obtained for his state through his own efforts under clarification (1)."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a National Review op-ed by Lieberman arguing for resolve in the war against Islamist extremism and against withdrawal from Iraq. It is pure position-taking/opinion writing with no claim of any delivered government benefit, excluded by negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of the 9/11 Commission bill and notes it 'contains McCaskill's amendment to provide whistleblower protection and collective bargaining rights for airport screeners.' Under clarification (3), announcing inclusion of her authored amendment in legislation that passed the chamber, framed as delivering benefits (plus Missouri's estimated $26.5 million in homeland-security funds), is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement at a Commerce Committee hearing praises and supports Vice Admiral Allen's nomination to be Coast Guard Commandant, but she does not claim to have recommended or secured the appointment. Under negative clarification (1), endorsements of nominees are pure position-taking, not credit-claiming. Clarification (4) would only apply if she claimed the nominee was her recommendation, which she does not."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Graham announces Senate passage (79-14) of the Farm Bill and claims personal credit for specific contents: 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs that could benefit research and training... in South Carolina' and the bill 'includes a Graham-backed provision' aiding textile manufacturers. Under clarification (3), announcing inclusion of provisions he championed in legislation that passed the chamber, framed as delivering benefits to his state, is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Reed announces Senate passage of the FY2008 budget resolution and highlights 'a provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund' included in it. Under clarification (3), announcing inclusion in advancing/passing legislation of a provision he championed, framed as delivering benefits, constitutes credit-claiming."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions applauds TVA's board decision to seek a license for the Bellefonte nuclear plant, saying it 'has been a priority of mine since I came to the Senate and I applaud the Board.' He claims no causal role in the decision. This parallels the negative example of Clinton welcoming an advisory board recommendation: applauding a favorable development plus general résumé language, excluded by negative clarifications (4) and (7)."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker announce a favorable Department of Transportation decision providing Essential Air Service between Jackson, TN and Cincinnati, and reference their own role: 'Senator Alexander and Senator Corker wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application.' Under clarification (2), claiming their intervention helped produce a favorable agency decision benefiting their state counts as credit-claiming; the announcement itself also associates them with the delivered benefit."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Domenici announces his role as original cosponsor of the newly introduced R-HoPE Act. Nothing has passed and no benefit has been delivered; under negative clarification (2), announcements of bill introductions/cosponsorships are proposals, not accomplishments. References to provisions he authored in the 2003 MMA are background résumé language in service of the introduction announcement (negative clarification (7)), not a new delivered benefit."
  }
}

{
  "50": {
    "document_id": "50",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement responds to President Bush's climate announcement by criticizing the administration's record ('head in the sand position,' 'failed spectacularly'). This is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration under negative clarification (1); Reid claims no personal responsibility for any delivered benefit or government action."
  },
  "57": {
    "document_id": "57",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy announces the introduction of the Human Cloning Ban and Stem Cell Research Protection Act with co-sponsors. Nothing has passed and no benefit has been delivered; this is a proposal, not an accomplishment. Under negative clarification (2), announcements of bill introductions or cosponsorships where nothing has passed are not credit-claiming. The references to potential benefits to Massachusetts research institutions are aspirational."
  },
  "89": {
    "document_id": "89",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dorgan announces that the Secretary of Energy designated Bismarck State College as the National Power Plant Operations Technology and Educational Center and explicitly notes he 'authored legislation, which was signed into law, that created the National Center designation.' This fits clarification (2)—claiming his authored law produced a favorable agency action (a federal designation benefiting his state)—and the announcement itself associates him with the delivered benefit."
  },
  "240": {
    "document_id": "240",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Ensign's statement on the Senate confirmation of Michael Chertoff simply praises the nominee and says he looks forward to working with him. There is no claim that Ensign recommended, secured, or caused the nomination or confirmation. Under negative clarification (1), this is position-taking/endorsement-type commentary with no claimed benefit."
  },
  "453": {
    "document_id": "453",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Nelson explains his votes against the Iraq withdrawal resolutions and articulates his preferred policy (measurable goals rather than arbitrary dates). This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: pure vote explanation/position-taking under negative clarification (1), with no delivered benefit claimed."
  },
  "597": {
    "document_id": "597",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lugar presents the Health and Fitness Award to community groups at his annual community run/walk. This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: ceremonial recognition of others under negative clarification (5), with no claim of responsibility for any government action or delivered benefit."
  },
  "656": {
    "document_id": "656",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement on the collapse of the Dubai Ports deal says 'the plug was mercifully pulled' and criticizes the Bush administration's incompetence on port security. He claims no causal role in the deal's demise; he merely welcomes the development and attacks the administration. This is welcoming a favorable development without claiming credit plus criticism of the administration—negative clarifications (4) and (1)."
  },
  "918": {
    "document_id": "918",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard's release simply announces a town meeting in Greeley and lists his committee assignments. This is a town-meeting announcement—ceremonial/logistical content under negative clarification (5)—with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit."
  },
  "967": {
    "document_id": "967",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline 'SHELBY ANNOUNCES OVER 3 MILLION FOR TALLADEGA' frames Shelby (noted as an Appropriations Committee member) as the bearer of $3,305,732 in HHS funds for Head Start programs in Alabama counties. Under clarification (1) and the rule that announcing a benefit for one's state counts even without the word 'secured,' this is credit-claiming for federal dollars directed to his state."
  },
  "1021": {
    "document_id": "1021",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline 'SENS. WYDEN, SMITH GAIN FUNDING APPROVAL FOR SALMON RECOVERY, OCEAN RESEARCH' frames the Appropriations Committee's approval of $13 million for the Pacific Coast Salmon Recovery Fund and $2 million for OSU as the senators' accomplishment in advancing legislation (the Senate FY2006 CJS bill). Under clarification (1) and (3), announcing appropriations for one's state included in advancing legislation, framed as a victory, is credit-claiming even though final passage has not yet occurred."
  },
  "1957": {
    "document_id": "1957",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dodd and Lieberman announce that Bradley International Airport will receive a $2,250,000 federal grant from the Department of Transportation for runway improvements, with quotes framing the benefit for Connecticut. Under clarification (1), announcing federal grants benefiting one's state counts as credit-claiming even without the word 'secured,' because the announcement associates the senators with the delivered benefit."
  },
  "2318": {
    "document_id": "2318",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is identical to the codebook's positive example. Although framed as a letter urging state action, Durbin explicitly claims past personal responsibility: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding.' Under clarification (2), explicit first-person claims of having produced favorable federal action count as credit-claiming."
  },
  "2807": {
    "document_id": "2807",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lincoln's release applauds the Arkansas Women in Agriculture proclamation signed by Governor Beebe and recognizes the contributions of Arkansas women farmers—ceremonial/commemorative content under negative clarification (5). The mention that she 'played a key role in the 2002 farm bill debate' is general résumé language not tied to any specific delivered benefit in a document whose purpose is ceremonial recognition, per negative clarification (7)."
  },
  "3482": {
    "document_id": "3482",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce a $294,980 federal SAFER grant to Allegany County firefighters, and the release notes Mikulski sits on the Homeland Security Appropriations Subcommittee that funds the program and 'fights each year to increase its federal funding.' Announcing federal grant dollars delivered to their state, with the announcement associating the senators with the benefit, fits clarification (1)—credit-claiming even without the word 'secured.'"
  },
  "3577": {
    "document_id": "3577",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sarbanes's committee statement criticizes FEMA's handling of the flood insurance program and calls for review of numerous issues. His reference to reforms 'that I worked with Senator Bunning and others on the Committee to formulate and move through' is used to complain that those reforms have NOT been implemented by FEMA—there is no delivered benefit being claimed. Under negative clarifications (1) and (7), this is position-taking/oversight criticism with incidental résumé language, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3659": {
    "document_id": "3659",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example exactly. Clinton 'welcomed' the NIOSH advisory board recommendation, urges Secretary Leavitt to act on it, and says she 'will continue to press' for Bethlehem Steel workers. Under negative clarification (4), applauding a favorable development without claiming a causal role, combined with advocacy for future action, is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3687": {
    "document_id": "3687",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bingaman announces that New Mexico State University has been awarded $599,691 from the USDA for food safety research, with his own quote touting the benefit. Under clarification (1) and the rule that announcing a federal benefit for one's state counts even without 'secured' language, this announcement associates the senator with delivered federal dollars to a state institution and is credit-claiming."
  },
  "4208": {
    "document_id": "4208",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dorgan and Lott announce they 'will introduce legislation' (the Media Ownership Act of 2007) to slow the FCC's media ownership rule changes. The bill has not passed and no benefit has been delivered; it is a proposal aimed at preventing future agency action. Under negative clarifications (2) and (3), bill introductions and advocacy urging future action are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4585": {
    "document_id": "4585",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Schumer announces that two Westchester County fire departments will receive $134,004 in federal Assistance to Firefighters grants, and the release highlights that 'Schumer has Pushed for Additional Funding' for the program. Announcing federal grant dollars delivered to his state, with the announcement associating the senator with the benefit, fits clarification (1)—credit-claiming even without the word 'secured.'"
  },
  "4771": {
    "document_id": "4771",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed around a meeting with the Schenectady Military Affairs Council, the release explicitly states that Clinton 'played a key role along with SMAC in preventing the removal of four C-130 Aircraft' from Stratton Air National Guard Base during the 2005 BRAC process, and quotes her pledge to keep fighting for the base. Under clarification (5), claiming credit for saving military missions, bases, or jobs counts as credit-claiming, and credit may be shared."
  }
}

{
  "817": {
    "document_id": "817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports that a subcommittee held a hearing on the Collins-Feinstein Social Security Fairness Act and describes Collins testifying in support of her bill. Nothing has passed and no benefit has been delivered; it is advocacy for pending legislation, which the negative clarification excludes (bill sponsorship/hearings and advocacy where no favorable outcome has occurred)."
  },
  "871": {
    "document_id": "871",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar questions Secretary Norton about proposed budget cuts to PILT, LWCF, and Park Service rules. This is oversight and criticism of the administration—pure position-taking under negative clarification (1)—with no claim of having delivered any benefit."
  },
  "1118": {
    "document_id": "1118",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Carper's statement explains his vote against the constitutional amendment banning gay marriage and articulates his reasoning. This is a vote explanation/position-taking, excluded by negative clarification (1); he claims no delivered benefit."
  },
  "1177": {
    "document_id": "1177",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Pryor and Chambliss call on the Air Force to reimburse unpaid subcontractors and announce they will continue to pressure the Secretary and pursue a legislative fix. This is advocacy and demands for future action, excluded by negative clarification (3); no favorable outcome has yet occurred."
  },
  "1230": {
    "document_id": "1230",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Harkin and Hatch welcome publication of the GMP regulations but explicitly tie the outcome to themselves: they were 'the principal authors' of DSHEA, which created the authority, and 'have been pushing the FDA to publish GMPs for more than a decade.' This matches clarification (2)—claiming their authored law and sustained pressure produced a favorable agency decision, specifically a published regulation. It goes beyond mere welcoming (negative clarification 4) because a causal role is asserted."
  },
  "1320": {
    "document_id": "1320",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Hutchison announces that Congress passed an emergency appropriations bill containing $3 billion for drought and wildfire relief benefiting Texas, saying 'I am pleased to help secure this funding.' This is a paradigmatic announcement of secured federal disaster-relief funds under clarification (1)."
  },
  "1747": {
    "document_id": "1747",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that the Senate approved legislation authored by Byrd ('legislation by Sen. Robert C. Byrd') boosting the National Guard Youth ChalleNGe program, including West Virginia's Mountaineer ChalleNGe, via his provision in the Defense Authorization Bill that passed 98-0. This is claiming credit for an authored provision that passed a chamber and delivers benefits (clarification 3)."
  },
  "1862": {
    "document_id": "1862",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Baucus and Tester announce $12.5 million for Butte military projects included in the Defense Appropriations Bill that passed the Appropriations Committee, stating they 'delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' and 'were proud to go to bat for these dollars.' This is classic credit-claiming for federal dollars directed to their state (clarification 1); shared credit between the two senators still counts."
  },
  "2026": {
    "document_id": "2026",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate Judiciary Committee passage of the Leahy-Specter Personal Data Privacy and Security Act, legislation Leahy introduced, framed as a victory that 'would help combat the devastating effects of identity theft' and deliver consumer protections. Under clarification (3), announcing passage/adoption in advancing legislation of a bill one authored, framed as delivering benefits, is credit-claiming; committee passage moves the bill to the full Senate."
  },
  "2627": {
    "document_id": "2627",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces that 'the Senate has approved his request for $1.5 million' for a University of Missouri facility—an explicit announcement that the senator personally secured federal funds for a state institution (clarification 1). This mirrors the codebook's own positive example."
  },
  "2675": {
    "document_id": "2675",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons describe meeting with BRAC commissioners to argue against closing Sub Base New London. The base has not been saved; Dodd says 'until we reach the finish line and hopefully prevail our work isn't done.' This is advocacy urging a future favorable decision, excluded by negative clarification (3)."
